theory M9d_CaptchaArtifactLogin
/*
  Model 9d - the CAPTCHA-artifact tier (Tier A / the recipe machinery). The
  browser mints the artifact a gated site demands - a solved CAPTCHA token,
  plus its own pre-session cookie jar - and the CORE still submits the
  credential over its own TLS. Only the session reaches the domain afterwards,
  exactly as in M9a.

  This model exists because M9a's proof assumes the desktop->site leg carries
  everything the site checks. Tier A breaks that assumption by design: the
  login request now contains a component the browser produced. The question
  the tier left open (`security/in-core-login-future-work.md`, §2, "M9d the
  formal model") is whether that component is an adversary-observable that
  CHANGES THE SECRECY STORY - whether letting the domain mint part of the
  login request lets the adversary learn the credential, or log in without it.

  The answer the model certifies: NO - expected inert. The token is modelled
  as fully adversary-observable (it is lifted out of a page, and pages are in
  the domain), and the cookie jar as fully adversary-chosen (In facts, the
  strongest form of attacker influence). Neither helps the adversary:

    * the token authenticates nothing about WHO logs in - the site checks it
      alongside a credential that comes only from the vault path;
    * the token is spent once (linear fact) - a lifted token cannot be replayed
      into a second login;
    * sessions are still minted exclusively behind the vault + approval path.

  What the model deliberately does NOT certify: the policy cost of the lift
  (the token shape is indistinguishable from CAPTCHA-relay tooling - recorded
  in the recipe module header, not hidden here), and anything about sites
  whose gate binds the login to the browser beyond the token (the Netflix-class
  wall, §4 of the future-work file - those sites are simply not reachable by
  this tier and not modelled).

  Expected:
    credential_never_leaks              VERIFIED - the answer to the open
        question: the observable artifact does not change the secrecy story.
    used_item_still_secret              VERIFIED - even after LoginOk.
    artifact_is_adversary_observable    (exists-trace) VERIFIED - the model
        really grants the adversary the token; the inertness result is not an
        artefact of hiding it.
    every_session_goes_through_the_vault VERIFIED - no session is minted
        without an approval of some vault credential at that origin.
    a_lifted_token_logs_in_once         VERIFIED - single-use: two logins can
        never share one token.
*/

begin

// The vault and the site's record of the SAME credential.
rule Setup:
    [ Fr(~cred) ]
  --[ Secret(~cred, $O) ]->
    [ !Vault(~cred, $O), !SiteStore(~cred, $O) ]

// One-time login approval (DE gate, M6/M9a-consistent).
rule Human_Approve_Login:
    [ !Vault(cred, $O), Fr(~g) ]
  --[ LoginApproved(cred, $O) ]->
    [ LoginGrant(cred, $O) ]

// The user solves the CAPTCHA in their OWN tab at the origin. The token is
// minted for the browser, and the page is in the domain, so the token goes Out:
// this rule deliberately HANDS IT TO THE ADVERSARY. That is the whole point -
// the open question was precisely whether this observability matters.
rule Human_Solves_Captcha:
    [ Fr(~tok) ]
  --[ CaptchaSolved($O, ~tok) ]->
    [ Out(<'captcha', $O, ~tok>), CaptchaToken($O, ~tok) ]

// The extension lifts the solved token out of the page and relays it to the
// core over native messaging (authenticated IPC - but the token was already
// public, so this leg adds no secrecy, only delivery).
rule Extension_Lifts_Token:
    [ CaptchaToken($O, tok) ]
  --[ TokenLifted($O, tok) ]->
    [ TokenAtCore($O, tok) ]

// Tier-A login: core submits credential + token over its own TLS. The cookie
// jar rides along as an In fact - fully adversary-chosen input, because the
// jar lives in the domain. It gates reachability at real sites; here the site
// checks none of it, which is the conservative direction: whatever influence
// the adversary gains over the request, the credential still comes from the
// vault path and the token is consumed (linear) by this very rule.
rule Desktop_Login_With_Artifact:
    [ !Vault(cred, $O), LoginGrant(cred, $O), TokenAtCore($O, tok), In(<'jar', $O>) ]
  --[ LoginPerformedWithArtifact(cred, $O, tok) ]->
    [ CredToSite(cred, $O, tok) ]

// The site verifies the credential against its record and issues the session
// artifact into the domain - same shape as M9a's issuance.
rule Site_CheckAndIssue:
    [ !SiteStore(cred, O), CredToSite(cred, O, tok), Fr(~sid) ]
  --[ SessionIssued(O, ~sid, tok), LoginOk(cred, O) ]->
    [ Out(<'session', O, ~sid>) ]

// ---- Secrecy: the open question, answered ------------------------------------

lemma credential_never_leaks:
  "All c O #i.
     Secret(c, O) @ i ==> not(Ex #j. K(c) @ j)"

lemma used_item_still_secret:
  "All c O #i.
     LoginOk(c, O) @ i ==> not(Ex #j. K(c) @ j)"

// ---- Structure: where sessions can come from, and how often tokens work ------

// Every session traces back to an approved vault credential at that origin.
// Possessing the token and the cookie jar - both adversary-reachable - mints
// nothing on its own.
lemma every_session_goes_through_the_vault:
  "All O sid t #i.
     SessionIssued(O, sid, t) @ i
     ==> Ex c #j. LoginApproved(c, O) @ j"

// Single-use: one token can never carry two logins. TokenAtCore is linear and
// nothing ever restores it, so a token lifted from the user's solve is spent
// exactly once - by the core, on the user's account.
lemma a_lifted_token_logs_in_once:
  "All c1 c2 O t #i #j.
     LoginPerformedWithArtifact(c1, O, t) @ i &
     LoginPerformedWithArtifact(c2, O, t) @ j
     ==> #i = #j"

// ---- Existence ----------------------------------------------------------------

// The premise of the open question, made true inside the model: the adversary
// really does learn the artifact. Everything above holds WITH this trace.
lemma artifact_is_adversary_observable:
  exists-trace
  "Ex O t #i. K(<'captcha', O, t>) @ i"

// Sanity: the tier still works when nobody attacks it.
lemma login_succeeds:
  exists-trace
  "Ex O sid c #i #j #k.
     LoginOk(c, O) @ i & K(<'session', O, sid>) @ j & LoginApproved(c, O) @ k"

end
